From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 14:33:05 +0200
Subject: net: dsa: lantiq: scope the xRX500 destination lookup per bridge

The switch forwards a frame by looking its destination address up in a
forwarding domain, and on this generation the domain that lookup runs in
is the one an ingress classification rule puts the frame in. A domain
reached through the port's default VLAN or through the VLAN tables takes
no part in it, which is why the common VLAN-table model cannot express a
forwarding domain here on its own: every port shares one, so a bridge
over some of the ports forwards between all of them and a standalone port
is not separated from a bridged one.

Give every user port a rule of its own instead. The rule matches the port
a frame arrived on and names its domain, so a station learned in one
domain is a lookup miss for a frame classified into another and the
unknown-destination map sends that frame to the processor rather than
across the fabric - the port ends up separated and still reachable, and
learning follows the domain because it is the same identifier.

A port outside every bridge takes a domain of its own so that it is
separated from every bridge and from every other standalone port; a port
in a bridge takes the bridge's. The identifiers come from the DSA core's
bridge numbering, which needs max_num_bridges to be set for the core to
hand out one number per bridge. Static address-table entries move with
it, since the domain the entry is planted in has to be the one the lookup
runs in.

A rule has to be written whole for either half of it to take effect. Its
key is a set of range pairs over the fields the parser extracts, and only
the first of them holds the ingress port; the range words the vendor's
encoder leaves in a rule that selects on the ingress port alone go in as
one set, because a key with the rest left at zero is stored and read back
exactly as written and matches nothing at all. Its action words are not
independent of one another either, so the action the vendor's encoder
builds is written whole: the port map its three gates hold inert, the
do-not-remark selectors that stop the VLAN action rewriting the frame's
priority fields, the alternative VLAN and domain select, and the tag
remark disables. Only the domain varies per port, so only the domain is
filled in by the writer. The learning-action enable goes in beside the
VLAN one, so the domain the rule assigns is the domain the port learns
in.

The per-model hook this hangs on is the one that applies a port's domain,
reached from port setup, from a join, from a leave, and from anywhere a
later re-initialisation needs it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

